      "text": "Third, due diligence. In the world of investments, due diligence is skimmed, but it is also the most exhausting part of legal work. Traditionally, teams of lawyers would spend weeks buried in documents, scanning leases, employment contracts and board minutes to identify hidden risks. With AI, I can upload entire data rooms and receive a structured summary of red flags in hours. I recall a case where AI highlighted that a critical intellectual property license was missing renewal, catching that early saved my client both money and future litigation. This does mean that AI replaces my judgment. It amplifies it, instead of drowning in paper, I can swim straight to the insights. Fourth, client education. Many of my clients are startup founders often brilliant technologies, but new to the world",
